{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No state rent control; municipal rent control authorized","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law establishing, governing or controlling rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, enforced by locally created boards.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rentals in municipalities that have adopted a rent control or rent leveling ordinance; tenants may ask the municipal rent control board or clerk whether their unit is covered.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Coverage depends on the local rent control ordinance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs landlord-tenant publication (Rent Control/Rent Increases)","quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after construction of the building is completed.","key_value":"30 years following completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in New Jersey, exempt from local rent control ordinances.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act requirements for rent increases","requirement":"A landlord may not raise the rent mid-lease; the old lease must be terminated, and the tenant offered a new lease at the increased rent by written notice. An increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies under the Anti-Eviction Act, including rentals subject to a municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"The increase must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases; an unconscionable increase may be challenged before a rent control board or a judge.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rent increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not prohibited.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to costs resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a dwelling unit used as a residence only by consent of the tenant or through the legal eviction process, and may not evict or fail to renew a lease without good cause proved in court.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; a Notice to Quit is required for all good cause evictions except eviction for nonpayment of rent.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer duties and withholding penalties","requirement":"A new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing it and returning it with interest. Willfully withholding a deposit from a tenant receiving public assistance is penalized.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 civil penalty per offense","coverage_conditions":"Security deposits for residential tenancies; the civil penalty applies where the tenant has received state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceedings; the State entity that made the deposits receives the penalty. Unlawful use of deposit monies may be charged as a disorderly person offense, with a fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense. The penalty shall be collected and enforced by summary proceedings pursuant to the Penalty Enforcement Law (N.J.S.A. 2A:58-12).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source-of-income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in the rental of housing because of the renter's source of lawful income; the LAD requires equal treatment in housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, as to familial status.","penalty":"Violators may have to pay monetary damages and rent the unit to the complainant; fines up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Credit report and rental application fee charges","requirement":"A landlord may charge the tenant for the cost of a credit or screening report and may request reasonable rental application fees. If an application is denied because of the report, the landlord must give the tenant the screening agency's name, address and telephone number.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Landlords screening prospective tenants; credit reports, tenant screening reports and background checks through public records.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"15 U.S.C.A. § 1681m","quoted_span":"If a tenant's application is denied due their credit report, the landlord must provide the tenant with the name, address, and telephone number of the credit reporting or screening agency that supplied the negative report (15 U.S.C.A. § 1681m). The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report.","interaction":"The denial-disclosure duty comes from the federal Fair Credit Reporting Act.","confidence":0.6,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
